1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,760
The previous episode found plane distance and
the closest point. We now resolve

2
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:11,000
the remaining problem: a point on the plane
may still lie outside the platform.

3
00:00:11,420 --> 00:00:16,620
First calculate triangle lengths and area,
then locate a point by proportions

4
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:19,160
of its three vertices. Distinguish off-plane
from outside-triangle points,

5
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:22,340
and use the same proportions to calculate
color.

6
00:00:22,983 --> 00:00:27,463
Give each edge the same index as its opposite
vertex.

7
00:00:27,703 --> 00:00:29,763
Adding the three edge lengths gives the
perimeter.

8
00:00:29,963 --> 00:00:35,323
A three-four-five right triangle has perimeter
twelve and semiperimeter six.

9
00:00:35,583 --> 00:00:38,923
Edge length divided by the sine of its
opposite

10
00:00:38,923 --> 00:00:42,303
angle is the same at all three vertices: the
sine law.

11
00:00:42,563 --> 00:00:46,763
Given two edges and their included angle,

12
00:00:46,983 --> 00:00:49,363
the cosine law finds the remaining edge using
squared lengths.

13
00:00:49,563 --> 00:00:52,463
Here three squared plus four squared equals
five squared.

14
00:00:52,463 --> 00:00:55,043
The right angle makes the cosine term zero.

15
00:00:55,423 --> 00:00:58,083
Do not mix degrees and radians.

16
00:00:58,283 --> 00:01:02,423
Validate positive lengths and triangle
feasibility after solving.

17
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:06,790
Observe platform ends and rock corners in
Noita.

18
00:01:07,050 --> 00:01:09,930
We can choose three visible locations as
reference points.

19
00:01:11,182 --> 00:01:15,702
Movement from one reference toward another
changes which point the character is nearer.

20
00:01:16,954 --> 00:01:18,854
Cartesian coordinates describe the location;

21
00:01:19,037 --> 00:01:22,854
three weights describe it relative to a
triangle that moves with its references.

22
00:01:24,126 --> 00:01:27,126
Track all three references when the camera
moves.

23
00:01:27,386 --> 00:01:31,366
Do not combine pixels from incompatible screen
frames.

24
00:01:31,366 --> 00:01:37,318
Choosing three points does not make the entire

25
00:01:37,818 --> 00:01:39,538
ground a triangle; gaps may lie between them.

26
00:01:40,990 --> 00:01:44,610
Our next example defines its own triangle,
calculates area,

27
00:01:44,610 --> 00:01:47,310
and expresses inside and outside positions
with three numbers.

28
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:50,210
We know the side lengths. Area will let us
describe

29
00:01:50,410 --> 00:01:54,910
a point in proportions of the three vertices.

30
00:01:55,490 --> 00:02:01,670
The normal example used six and four. Compare
the three area formulas on a

31
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:06,370
separate four-by-three triangle; return to six
and four for the weights.

32
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:13,466
A turret near a floor portal is drawn into the
device. Red marks the visible floor boundary.

33
00:02:13,651 --> 00:02:17,911
Knowing plane orientation differs from knowing
the finite passage region.

34
00:02:18,337 --> 00:02:23,417
In the next corridor, moving floor and wall
portals changes the panels' path.

35
00:02:23,652 --> 00:02:26,532
Hide geometry while debris obscures the edges;

36
00:02:26,672 --> 00:02:30,568
distinguish corners and interior positions
when the surface returns.

37
00:02:31,383 --> 00:02:38,197
In co-op, portals lead to a finite platform.
Now use area to locate an interior point.

38
00:02:38,817 --> 00:02:43,617
Use vertices zero, zero; four, zero; and zero,
three.

39
00:02:43,797 --> 00:02:47,417
Base four times height three divided by two
gives area six.

40
00:02:47,617 --> 00:02:51,617
With side lengths three, four and five,

41
00:02:51,837 --> 00:02:55,317
Heron formula uses semiperimeter six and its
differences from each edge.

42
00:02:55,617 --> 00:02:58,277
The radicand is six times three times two
times one,

43
00:02:58,277 --> 00:03:00,997
or thirty-six. Its square root is six.

44
00:03:01,317 --> 00:03:07,077
The cross-product length is the parallelogram
area. Half gives the triangle area.

45
00:03:07,077 --> 00:03:12,917
Here the parallelogram area is twelve, and
dividing by two gives a triangle area of six.

46
00:03:13,023 --> 00:03:16,137
In2D, the corresponding determinant has a
sign.

47
00:03:16,517 --> 00:03:20,237
Reversing winding changes the sign but not
area magnitude.

48
00:03:20,353 --> 00:03:23,677
Taking a3D cross-product magnitude discards
direction.

49
00:03:23,937 --> 00:03:26,957
Distinguish area from front-face orientation.

50
00:03:27,583 --> 00:03:33,643
The earlier midpoint mixed two positions
equally.

51
00:03:33,863 --> 00:03:37,263
Extend that idea to three vertices, returning
to the original six-by-four triangle.

52
00:03:37,483 --> 00:03:43,683
Twenty, thirty and fifty percent total one
hundred percent.

53
00:03:43,963 --> 00:03:48,403
Multiply each position by its proportion and
add.

54
00:03:48,663 --> 00:03:51,523
These are barycentric coordinates; the name
does not require real masses.

55
00:03:52,150 --> 00:03:58,110
Barycentric coordinates give weights for three
vertices. Their sum must be one.

56
00:03:58,310 --> 00:04:01,650
Multiply each vertex by its weight and add.

57
00:04:01,890 --> 00:04:04,430
The first vertex has coordinates one, zero,
zero.

58
00:04:04,670 --> 00:04:08,690
The midpoint of the first two vertices is one
half, one half,

59
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:11,970
zero. Equal one-third weights give the vertex
average.

60
00:04:12,150 --> 00:04:15,930
Although written as three numbers, only two
are independent because their sum is fixed.

61
00:04:16,310 --> 00:04:18,510
A triangle surface is two-dimensional.

62
00:04:18,510 --> 00:04:23,090
Using the third vertex as origin gives axes
first-minus-third and second-minus-third.

63
00:04:23,470 --> 00:04:25,910
They need not be orthogonal or unit length.

64
00:04:26,250 --> 00:04:30,690
On a nondegenerate triangle plane, nonnegative
weights describe its interior and boundary.

65
00:04:31,030 --> 00:04:34,310
Negative weights meaningfully describe outside
points.

66
00:04:35,083 --> 00:04:38,323
Connecting to another rock changes the marking
screen position.

67
00:04:38,403 --> 00:04:41,703
Choose three reference points around the
attachment marking.

68
00:04:43,668 --> 00:04:47,328
Weights relative to the same three vertices
provide a

69
00:04:47,328 --> 00:04:50,548
location reference even as a surface or camera
moves.

70
00:04:50,748 --> 00:04:57,693
Approaching a vertex makes its weight approach
the number one.

71
00:04:58,253 --> 00:05:01,673
A point on its opposite edge has zero weight
for it.

72
00:05:03,837 --> 00:05:09,297
Screen proximity is not automatically the3D
barycentric

73
00:05:09,297 --> 00:05:11,797
relationship because perspective changes the
projection.

74
00:05:14,342 --> 00:05:18,262
We have not extracted the real attachment
coordinates.

75
00:05:18,562 --> 00:05:22,442
Visible markings motivate the explicitly
defined numerical example.

76
00:05:24,807 --> 00:05:27,347
Now we will multiply vertices by weights and
see

77
00:05:27,607 --> 00:05:31,947
how a negative weight places a point outside.

78
00:05:32,754 --> 00:05:36,987
Define3D vertices zero, zero, zero; six, zero,

79
00:05:36,987 --> 00:05:40,787
zero; and zero, four, zero. All lie in the z
zero plane.

80
00:05:41,027 --> 00:05:44,727
Weights point two, point three and point five
sum to one.

81
00:05:44,967 --> 00:05:48,967
Compute x as six times point three and y as
four times point five.

82
00:05:49,207 --> 00:05:54,707
The result is one point eight, two, zero. All
weights are nonnegative, so it lies inside.

83
00:05:54,947 --> 00:05:58,887
Now use minus point two, point seven and point
five.

84
00:05:59,307 --> 00:06:02,047
Their sum is still one, but the first weight
is negative.

85
00:06:02,047 --> 00:06:08,847
The result four point two, two, zero lies
beyond the edge opposite the first vertex.

86
00:06:09,007 --> 00:06:12,547
Position has length units; weights are
dimensionless ratios. Rejecting every

87
00:06:12,707 --> 00:06:18,267
negative weight discards information
describing valid outside positions.

88
00:06:18,933 --> 00:06:22,193
Weights constructed one point eight, two,
zero.

89
00:06:22,473 --> 00:06:27,513
Recover the weights from that same point and
triangle.

90
00:06:27,933 --> 00:06:33,933
The six-by-four triangle has area twelve.
Joining the point to the vertices gives

91
00:06:33,933 --> 00:06:38,693
subareas two point four, three point six and
six. Divide by twelve to recover point two,

92
00:06:38,833 --> 00:06:42,473
point three and point five. Keep signed areas
to handle outside points.

93
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:46,420
Given a position, obtain each weight from a
signed

94
00:06:46,620 --> 00:06:50,960
subtriangle area divided by the whole signed
area.

95
00:06:51,220 --> 00:06:58,980
For the inside example, the subareas are
twenty, thirty and fifty percent of the total.

96
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:03,520
Keep consistent vertex order and signed areas.

97
00:07:03,940 --> 00:07:06,320
Absolute areas discard the negative weight of
an outside point.

98
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:09,720
Crossing the edge opposite the first vertex
reverses

99
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:12,460
that subarea sign and makes its weight
negative.

100
00:07:12,460 --> 00:07:16,520
Reversing all winding reverses numerator and
denominator together,

101
00:07:16,520 --> 00:07:19,620
preserving weights. Be consistent.

102
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:22,340
A zero whole area cannot be divided by.

103
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:27,020
Very thin triangles may amplify small
coordinate errors.

104
00:07:27,783 --> 00:07:33,003
The character moves among ledges at different
heights.

105
00:07:33,203 --> 00:07:37,319
Distinguish visual overlap from occupying the
same surface.

106
00:07:37,475 --> 00:07:40,639
This2D footage exposes two position
components; in3D,

107
00:07:40,686 --> 00:07:44,739
different depths may project to the same
screen location.

108
00:07:46,335 --> 00:07:48,995
An apparently inside projected location alone
does

109
00:07:48,995 --> 00:07:51,475
not prove that a3D query lies on a triangle
surface.

110
00:07:52,991 --> 00:07:58,671
Separate distance-to-plane validation from
within-plane barycentric tests.

111
00:07:58,671 --> 00:08:04,767
Passing a platform edge can also leave its
finite footprint while

112
00:08:05,227 --> 00:08:07,927
keeping the same reference height. Remember
negative outside weights.

113
00:08:09,563 --> 00:08:15,163
Our diagram will compare two points with
identical projection and different depths.

114
00:08:15,783 --> 00:08:18,683
We found a triangle address. Viewed straight
on,

115
00:08:18,683 --> 00:08:21,943
a point away from the plane can appear at the
same position.

116
00:08:22,203 --> 00:08:27,243
The triangle lies at z zero. One point eight,
two, zero and one point eight, two,

117
00:08:27,243 --> 00:08:33,083
seven share x and y, but the second is seven
units off-plane. Test projected address and

118
00:08:33,083 --> 00:08:39,323
coplanarity separately. The shadow analogy
explains position overlap, not lighting.

119
00:08:39,950 --> 00:08:47,150
Our inside point was one point eight, two,
zero. Keep x and y but change z to seven.

120
00:08:47,390 --> 00:08:50,430
Both points have the same XY projection,

121
00:08:50,730 --> 00:08:54,490
but the new point is seven units off the
triangle plane.

122
00:08:54,750 --> 00:08:58,630
For a projected solve, dropping the axis with
largest absolute

123
00:08:58,630 --> 00:09:02,130
normal component helps choose a
well-conditioned projection.

124
00:09:02,370 --> 00:09:05,130
The discarded coordinate still matters.

125
00:09:05,370 --> 00:09:09,930
Check plane distance separately before testing
projected weights.

126
00:09:09,950 --> 00:09:14,950
Plane proximity tolerance has spatial units
and scale;

127
00:09:15,310 --> 00:09:18,390
barycentric tolerance uses dimensionless
ratios.

128
00:09:18,690 --> 00:09:22,770
Also validate finite inputs, normal length and
the area denominator.

129
00:09:22,990 --> 00:09:26,210
An inside test is not complete input
validation.

130
00:09:27,090 --> 00:09:29,770
The turret rises from the floor toward the
device.

131
00:09:30,048 --> 00:09:35,248
Apparent overlap with the surface below does
not mean it occupies that same plane point.

132
00:09:35,527 --> 00:09:37,747
Different colors distinguish the objects.

133
00:09:38,085 --> 00:09:43,831
In the next bright room, a floor portal
precedes the robot rising.

134
00:09:44,157 --> 00:09:48,457
A triangle traced on visible tile corners is
an illustrative projected shape,

135
00:09:48,764 --> 00:09:54,930
not the actual game mesh. Test coplanarity and
triangle inclusion separately.

136
00:09:55,550 --> 00:09:59,290
We move among narrow ledges and slanted
terrain in Noita.

137
00:09:59,530 --> 00:10:02,310
Track three nearby corners together.

138
00:10:03,554 --> 00:10:08,734
A thin reference triangle can turn a small
position change into a large weight change.

139
00:10:08,994 --> 00:10:14,678
Producing three numbers does not guarantee
numerical

140
00:10:14,938 --> 00:10:17,338
stability for nearly zero area. Inspect the
input shape as well.

141
00:10:18,462 --> 00:10:23,382
Changing reference vertices changes the
position represented by the same weights.

142
00:10:23,662 --> 00:10:25,842
Coordinates and their triangle form one
description.

143
00:10:25,842 --> 00:10:30,826
Real boundaries contain notches and gaps;

144
00:10:31,186 --> 00:10:35,386
filling a mathematical triangle between
references does not reproduce the terrain.

145
00:10:36,430 --> 00:10:39,670
Today concerns one plane and triangle.

146
00:10:39,850 --> 00:10:43,930
The next lecture partitions more complex
boundaries into triangles.

147
00:10:44,550 --> 00:10:50,090
After checking coplanarity and triangle
inclusion, determine the value at that

148
00:10:50,510 --> 00:10:54,390
location. Reuse the address we just found
rather than construct new coordinates.

149
00:10:54,632 --> 00:10:59,270
Store red, green and blue values at the
vertices instead of positions.

150
00:10:59,690 --> 00:11:06,690
Multiply by the same weights and add to obtain
an intermediate color. This is a simple

151
00:11:07,090 --> 00:11:11,290
linear color example, not a claim about every
displayed color or a game's implementation.

152
00:11:12,233 --> 00:11:15,113
Barycentric coordinates combine attributes as
well as positions.

153
00:11:15,201 --> 00:11:19,593
Assign different values to the three vertices.

154
00:11:19,833 --> 00:11:26,013
Give the vertices red, green and blue in an
explicitly linear color example.

155
00:11:26,293 --> 00:11:33,153
Weights point two, point three and point five
produce those same three color components.

156
00:11:33,673 --> 00:11:40,253
An edge midpoint averages its endpoints; a
vertex reproduces its own attribute.

157
00:11:40,253 --> 00:11:46,073
The same construction interpolates defined
heights or texture coordinates.

158
00:11:46,753 --> 00:11:49,733
Direction attributes that require
normalization need a further check.

159
00:11:50,073 --> 00:11:52,493
This is affine interpolation on a triangle
surface.

160
00:11:53,033 --> 00:11:57,733
Perspective-correct interpolation in screen
space belongs to the rendering chapter.

161
00:11:58,367 --> 00:12:01,727
Contact the blue-coated platform and move on.

162
00:12:01,987 --> 00:12:07,824
Separate the question of surface location from
the value stored at that location.

163
00:12:08,442 --> 00:12:11,182
Another excerpt shows a cube, followed by
airborne travel.

164
00:12:11,656 --> 00:12:14,936
To read color, first identify the point on the
surface.

165
00:12:15,470 --> 00:12:17,890
Contact with the blue wall leads to a bounce.

166
00:12:18,150 --> 00:12:20,897
The gel effect is not evidence of color
interpolation.

167
00:12:21,717 --> 00:12:25,217
Observe differently colored roofs and walls in
Serious Sam2.

168
00:12:25,537 --> 00:12:30,077
Appearance varies with surface location.

169
00:12:32,196 --> 00:12:35,096
Finding a surface position and then finding
its

170
00:12:35,096 --> 00:12:37,956
attributes are two connected graphics
questions.

171
00:12:40,095 --> 00:12:44,955
If values are stored per triangle vertex, our
weights construct intermediate values.

172
00:12:45,235 --> 00:12:47,335
This is the mathematical model we explicitly
defined.

173
00:12:49,335 --> 00:12:53,855
The real image may involve textures, lighting
and effects.

174
00:12:53,855 --> 00:12:59,734
Footage alone does not confirm vertex-color
interpolation.

175
00:13:00,134 --> 00:13:03,574
Preserve surface references as the camera
moves.

176
00:13:04,094 --> 00:13:07,454
Do not mix screen coordinates with
object-surface coordinates.

177
00:13:09,713 --> 00:13:13,853
Our final problems validate plane distance and
triangle weights

178
00:13:14,233 --> 00:13:17,693
together: locate the point, then calculate its
attributes.

179
00:13:18,433 --> 00:13:23,973
First problem: the normal has components zero,
two, zero, and constant d equals four.

180
00:13:24,153 --> 00:13:27,773
What is the distance from this plane to point
four, five, minus three?

181
00:13:28,033 --> 00:13:32,293
Residual six divided by normal length two
gives distance three.

182
00:13:32,393 --> 00:13:35,653
The closest plane point is four, two, minus
three.

183
00:13:35,913 --> 00:13:39,793
Second: on our z zero triangle, weights point
two,

184
00:13:39,793 --> 00:13:43,813
point three and point five construct one point
eight, two, zero.

185
00:13:44,213 --> 00:13:47,933
Changing only z to seven preserves the
projection but leaves the plane.

186
00:13:47,933 --> 00:13:51,393
It must not pass a triangle-surface membership
test.

187
00:13:51,813 --> 00:13:56,213
Check valid inputs, plane distance, triangle
weights and desired attributes in that order,

188
00:13:56,213 --> 00:14:00,293
with appropriate units and tolerances.

189
00:14:00,593 --> 00:14:06,573
We connected plane orientation and location,
distance and projection, triangle area

190
00:14:06,973 --> 00:14:10,873
and barycentric coordinates. Next we will
compare centers and partition polygons.
